-- Vocabulary V3 step by step (IELTS-V3 v1.1.0): five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer). Auto-marked, so filed as reading tasks; every item is
-- mapped to the vocabulary subskill it practises. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 13:01 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'.
-- Named-student pilot with requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json.
do $steps$
declare art constant text := '

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

THE PASSAGE: Who should pay for university?

A. In many countries, the question of whether university education should be free at the point of use has become one of the most divisive issues in education policy. Supporters of free tuition argue that higher education benefits society as a whole, and that charging fees discourages talented students from poorer families from applying. Opponents respond that graduates themselves are the main beneficiaries, since a degree tends to increase lifetime earnings, and that it is therefore unfair to ask taxpayers who did not attend university to cover the cost. In my view, both positions contain an element of truth, but neither is as straightforward as its advocates suggest.

B. Consider first the claim that fees deter disadvantaged students. It seems intuitively plausible, and in some countries the introduction of fees was followed by a fall in applications. Yet the evidence is more mixed than is often assumed. In several systems where fees are high but are repaid only once a graduate''s income exceeds a certain threshold, participation among poorer students has continued to rise. What appears to matter most is not the price itself but whether students believe they will be able to manage the debt. Clear information about repayment, I would argue, may do as much to widen access as abolishing fees altogether.

C. The argument that graduates should pay because they benefit personally is also less convincing than it first appears. It is true that, on average, graduates earn more than non-graduates, but averages conceal enormous variation between subjects and institutions. Moreover, many of the professions on which society depends most, such as nursing and teaching, require degrees but offer relatively modest salaries. Requiring these graduates to repay large debts may discourage people from entering exactly the careers that a country most needs to fill.

D. There is also a practical consideration. Free tuition must be funded from general taxation, and when public budgets are under pressure, governments that have abolished fees frequently respond by limiting the number of university places. The result can be that access becomes more restricted, not less, since the available places tend to go to applicants from schools that prepare students most effectively for competitive entry.

E. A more defensible position, in my opinion, lies between the two extremes. Fees could be retained for most courses but reduced or waived for subjects linked to shortages of skilled workers, and repayments could be linked to income so that no graduate is required to pay more than they can reasonably afford. Such a system would not satisfy those who regard free education as a matter of principle. It would, however, address the two concerns that matter most: ensuring that cost does not prevent capable students from applying, and ensuring that public money is directed where it brings the greatest benefit.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r70-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 1 of 6: Meet the words','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the meaning of each word or phrase.','Precise words say exactly what you mean: ''stem from'' is a cause, ''tackle'' is action, ''outweigh'' is a comparison.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "What does ''stem from'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  cause something to happen"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  be more important than"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  put a plan into action"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  be the result of"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "What does ''tackle'' mean in ''tackle a problem''?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make it worse"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  talk about it without acting"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  ignore it"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  take action to deal with it"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "What does ''have a detrimental effect on'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  be caused by"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  cause harm to"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  have no effect on"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  be good for"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "What does ''outweigh'' mean in ''The benefits outweigh the drawbacks''?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  be more important than"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  be the same as"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  be heavier in kilograms than"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  be the result of"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "What is a ''drawback''?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a cause"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  an advantage"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a disadvantage"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a solution"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "What does ''implement'' mean in ''implement a policy''?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  write about it"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  put it into action"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  cancel it"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  criticise it"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 6, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 D, ''Stem from'' = be the result of: the problem comes FROM the cause.\n2 D, ''Tackle'' (or ''address'') a problem = take action to deal with it.\n3 B, ''Detrimental'' = harmful: noise has a detrimental effect on concentration.\n4 A, ''Outweigh'' = be greater or more important than. The benefits matter more.\n5 C, A ''drawback'' (or ''downside'') is a disadvantage.\n6 B, ''Implement'' (or ''introduce'') a policy = put the plan into action.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Choose precise vocabulary: learn the meaning of precise Task 2 words for causes, effects, solutions and evaluation."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: precise words for arguments

Task 2 essays explain causes, effects and solutions, and weigh up two sides. Precise words make this clear.
- lead to / result in = cause
- stem from / be caused by = be the result of
- tackle / address a problem = take action to deal with it
- implement a policy = put it into action
- have a detrimental effect on = cause harm to
- outweigh = be more important than
- drawback / downside = a disadvantage

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Many health problems stem from poor diet.''
Which comes first? Poor diet (the cause) -> health problems (the result).
So ''stem from'' points BACK to the cause. ''Lead to'' points FORWARD to the result: ''Poor diet can lead to health problems.''

Now choose the meaning of six words.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'20ee5e7d9718b8291d4ac19eaa0a4863ec1743e44741618904da915962a2494f',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r71-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 2 of 6: The right small word','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the word that completes each phrase correctly.','Learn the phrase as one piece: lead TO, result IN, stem FROM, an effect ON, beneficial TO, caused BY, a key factor IN.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Poor sleep can result ______ lower marks at school.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  from"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  in"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  on"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  to"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Low wages are a key factor ______ the shortage of nurses.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  at"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  in"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  to"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  of"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Loud music in restaurants can have a detrimental effect ______ conversation.", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  at"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  in"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  to"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  on"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Many accidents are caused ______ drivers using their phones.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  with"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  from"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  by"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  of"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The city plans to ______ a new recycling scheme next year.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  implement"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  outweigh"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  stem"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  result"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Rising rents can lead ______ homelessness.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  to"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  in"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  from"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  on"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 B, in: ''result in'' is followed by the consequence, so ''result in lower marks''.\n2 B, in: ''a key factor in'' is followed by the situation, so ''a key factor in the shortage''.\n3 D, on: we have an effect ON something, so ''a detrimental effect on conversation''.\n4 C, by: in the passive, ''be caused by'' is followed by the cause, so ''caused by drivers using their phones''.\n5 A, implement: put a scheme into action. The other verbs do not take ''a scheme'' as their object.\n6 A, to: ''lead to'' is followed by the result, so ''lead to homelessness''.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Use natural collocation: choose the correct preposition or verb in fixed academic phrases."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: learn the whole phrase

Many marks are lost on one small word. Learn these as whole phrases:
- lead TO
- result IN
- stem FROM
- be caused BY
- have an effect ON
- be beneficial TO
- a key factor IN
- tackle / address a problem
- implement a policy

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Rising house prices have resulted ______ more young adults living with their parents.''
''Result'' takes ''in'': have resulted IN. (''Led to'' would also be possible, but not ''resulted to''.)

Now choose six words.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'f7633a63d8b7d190b67009ce8c0db90115eb62c821eff57fa0673d8c30f23207',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r72-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 3 of 6: Careful, precise claims','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the meaning of each careful claim, or the most careful and precise sentence.','Strong essays make claims they can defend: arguably, to some extent, it is likely that, a significant proportion of.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "''I agree with this view to some extent.'' The writer ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6daeb023-a3c8-49ca-961a-008bdcc94e39", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  has no opinion"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  does not agree at all"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  partly agrees"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  completely agrees"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "''It is unlikely that cars will disappear soon.'' This means ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6daeb023-a3c8-49ca-961a-008bdcc94e39", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  cars will probably not disappear soon"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  cars will certainly not disappear soon"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  nobody knows anything about cars"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  cars will soon disappear"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "''A significant proportion of graduates work abroad.'' This means ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6daeb023-a3c8-49ca-961a-008bdcc94e39", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  all graduates work abroad"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  exactly half of graduates work abroad"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a few graduates work abroad"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a large part of graduates work abroad"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Which sentence makes the most careful claim that can be defended?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "6daeb023-a3c8-49ca-961a-008bdcc94e39", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Everybody knows online learning is better."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Online learning is obviously the best way to learn."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Arguably, online learning suits some students better than classrooms do."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Online learning is better for all students."}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Which sentence is the most precise?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Long working hours can lead to stress-related illness."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Work causes lots of problems and things."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Working a lot is bad for people."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  It is a very big problem for many people."}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Which sentence is the most precise?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  People cycle a lot nowadays."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  A significant number of commuters now cycle to work."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Many many people cycle."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  A lot of people do cycling."}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 C, partly agrees: ''to some extent'' = partly. (''To a large extent'' = mostly.)\n2 A, probably not: ''unlikely'' is careful, not certain.\n3 D, a large part: ''a significant proportion'' is many, but not all and not an exact figure.\n4 C, ''Arguably ... some students'' is careful and limited. The others claim too much: ''all'', ''everybody'', ''obviously''.\n5 A, It names the cause (long working hours) and the effect (stress-related illness). The others are vague.\n6 B, ''A significant number of commuters ... cycle to work'' says exactly who and what. The others are vague or repetitive.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Control register and tone: choose hedged, precise claims rather than vague or exaggerated ones."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: claims you can defend

An examiner rewards claims that are careful and precise, not extreme or vague.
- arguably = a careful claim you can defend
- to some extent = partly; to a large extent = mostly
- it is likely / unlikely that = probably / probably not
- a significant number / proportion of = many / a large part of (more precise than ''a lot of'')

WORKED EXAMPLE
Too strong: ''Online learning will replace schools.''
Vague: ''Online learning is a big thing now.''
Careful and precise: ''It is unlikely that online learning will replace schools entirely, although it may change how they teach.''

Now choose six careful claims.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'831c86712131c6a61bced633e7cdcdabb60b16fb7f6eb34b77154bcda1ab465a',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r73-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 4 of 6: Although, despite, however','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the word or phrase that makes each concession correct.','Although + a clause (subject + verb). Despite + a noun. However starts a new sentence, after a full stop or semicolon.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "______ the cost, many students choose to study abroad.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Admittedly"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Despite"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Although"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  However"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "______ fees are high, most graduates earn more over their lifetime.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Despite"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Because of"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Although"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  However"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Admittedly, fees can put some students off. ______, clear repayment rules can reduce this effect.", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Despite"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Because"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Although"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  However"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Despite ______, the policy was introduced.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  it was opposed"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  people opposed it"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  strong opposition"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  oppose strongly"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Although the scheme ______, it has already reduced traffic.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  is expensive"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  expensively"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  being expensive"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  expense"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Which sentence is correct?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  However the benefits are clear, some people remain unconvinced."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Although the benefits are clear, some people remain unconvinced."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Although the clear benefits, some people remain unconvinced."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Despite the benefits are clear, some people remain unconvinced."}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 B, Despite: ''the cost'' is a noun, so it needs ''despite''.\n2 C, Although: ''fees are high'' is a clause (subject + verb).\n3 D, However: after ''Admittedly'', the counter-argument starts a new sentence with ''However''.\n4 C, strong opposition: ''despite'' needs a noun phrase, not a clause.\n5 A, is expensive: ''although'' needs a clause with a verb.\n6 B, ''Although'' + clause is correct. ''Despite'' cannot take a clause, ''although'' cannot take only a noun, and ''however'' does not join two clauses like this.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Recognise logical relationships: use concession language with the correct grammar."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: accept a point, then argue against it

A strong argument admits the other side, then answers it. The grammar matters:
- ALTHOUGH + clause: Although fees are high, a degree usually pays for itself.
- DESPITE + noun: Despite the high fees, a degree usually pays for itself.
- ADMITTEDLY ... HOWEVER: Admittedly, fees are high. However, a degree usually pays for itself.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''______ the cost, many families choose private education.''
After the gap comes ''the cost'': a noun, not a clause. -> Despite

Now choose six words or phrases.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'02c9c8a2050cce4663c216b475ebdd913efff8ce137b8667f36e3f7dc8cfa970',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r74-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 5 of 6: How the writer signals a view','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six questions about how the writer of the passage signals claims, concessions and conclusions.','Signals show the writer''s moves: In my view (own position), Yet (disagreement), It is true that ... but (concession), I would argue (careful claim).','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "In paragraph A, which phrase shows where the writer''s own view begins?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  has become one of the most divisive issues"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Opponents respond"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Supporters of free tuition argue"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  In my view"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "In paragraph B, which word shows that the writer is about to disagree with a common belief?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Yet"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Consider"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  followed"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  first"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "In paragraph B, ''I would argue'' shows that the writer is ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  stating a proven fact"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  quoting a study"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  making a careful claim of their own"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  reporting what students say"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "In paragraph C, ''It is true that ... but'' is used to ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  end the essay"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  accept a point and then limit it"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  reject a point completely"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  give an example"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "In paragraph D, ''The result can be that'' introduces ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  an example"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  a certain consequence"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a possible consequence"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a cause"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "In paragraph E, the writer says such a system ''would not satisfy'' some people but ''would, however, address'' two concerns. This shows the writer ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  has no clear opinion"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  changes their mind"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  rejects the system"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  admits a drawback but still supports the system"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 D, In my view: \"In my view, both positions contain an element of truth\". The other phrases report other people or describe the debate.\n2 A, Yet: \"It seems intuitively plausible ... Yet the evidence is more mixed than is often assumed.\"\n3 C, making a careful claim of their own: \"Clear information about repayment, I would argue, may do as much to widen access\". ''May'' keeps it careful.\n4 B, accept a point and then limit it: \"It is true that, on average, graduates earn more than non-graduates, but averages conceal enormous variation\".\n5 C, a possible consequence: ''can be'' shows possibility. \"The result can be that access becomes more restricted\".\n6 D, admits a drawback but still supports the system: \"Such a system would not satisfy those who regard free education as a matter of principle. It would, however, address the two concerns that matter most\".\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Identify viewpoint: recognise how a writer signals claims, concessions and conclusions in an argument."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: signals show the moves

In an argument text the writer uses signals to show each move:
- own position: in my view, in my opinion, I would argue
- reporting others: supporters argue, opponents respond
- disagreement: yet, however, but
- concession: it is true that ... but, admittedly
- possible effect: can, may, the result can be that

WORKED EXAMPLE
Paragraph A: "Supporters of free tuition argue that higher education benefits society as a whole".
''Supporters ... argue'' reports OTHER people''s view. It is not the writer''s own position.

Now answer six questions.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'437d1d0da7eb125039101d87a4e8c9c7ca970194706d25667040f82dcfdaa7a2',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r75-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V3 step 6 of 6: Mixed check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six new questions on the passage. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Use the signals to find the writer''s view, check how strong it is, and work out word meaning from the sentence around it.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The writer thinks that the arguments on both sides are simpler than they appear.", "accepted_answers": ["NO"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The writer thinks clear information about repayment could help more students go to university.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The writer thinks nurses should be paid higher salaries.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "In paragraph C, ''averages conceal enormous variation'' means ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e55dff22-7ea2-4bb6-b724-7d129b554bb4", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  salaries are the same in every subject"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  averages hide very large differences"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  averages are always wrong"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  averages show every difference clearly"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "In paragraph E, ''waived'' is closest in meaning to ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  not charged"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explained"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  paid later"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  increased"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "The writer believes repayments should depend on how much graduates earn.", "accepted_answers": ["YES"], "taxonomy_node_id": "3cc4beec-8c09-4289-bd3d-185f5319240a", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "YES", "label": "YES"}, {"value": "NO", "label": "NO"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 NO. Proof: \"neither is as straightforward as its advocates suggest\". The writer thinks they are less simple than they appear.\n2 YES. Proof: \"Clear information about repayment, I would argue, may do as much to widen access as abolishing fees altogether.\"\n3 NOT GIVEN. The writer says nursing offers \"relatively modest salaries\", but never says whether salaries should rise.\n4 B, averages hide very large differences: to conceal = to hide; the differences are \"between subjects and institutions\".\n5 A, not charged: fees \"reduced or waived\" for shortage subjects means lowered or removed.\n6 YES. Proof: in the writer''s position, \"repayments could be linked to income so that no graduate is required to pay more than they can reasonably afford\".\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "YES/NO/NOT GIVEN and precise word meaning in an argument text."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole routine

1. Find the writer''s OWN view (in my view, I would argue), not other people''s.
2. Check how strong it is (may, can, could, arguably).
3. For a word, read the sentence around it and choose the meaning that fits.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Statement: ''The writer thinks the evidence about fees and poorer students is simple.''
Proof: "Yet the evidence is more mixed than is often assumed."  -> NO

Now answer six new questions on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'9a0de414f1428fcc98e91abbb505d2591f2eba90c8b77e9718edb66f3325c194',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
end $steps$;
